  "raw": "\n\nMonday marks four years since Gov. Jerry Brown declared California's first statewide drought emergency, although he didn't use that term last week during a stop in downtown Los Angeles.\n\nThe designation allowed farmers to request emergency water from local regulators and preserved water rights or funding for projects designed to prevent evaporation and drain storage reservoirs.\n\nBrown says parts of Californi higher mountains still face severe and persistent drought conditions., and rains are needed soon. The governor decided that 164 of California's technical drought criteria had been met, leaving just 42 out of 415 population districts with severe problems. Those remote areas have not received recent precipitation.\n\nThe weather service reported 10 inches (25 centimeters) of new fall rainfall at an area atop Buttermilk Mountain north of Sacramento this week alone. According to the Gold Country Tribune newspaper, another 50 inches (1.5 meters) may accumulate by summer's end. Lake Oroville is America's tallest man-made lake, nearly 140 feet (44",
  "serialized": "Monday marks four years since Gov. Jerry Brown declared California's first statewide drought emergency, although he didn't use that term last week during a stop in downtown Los Angeles. The designation allowed farmers to request emergency water from local regulators and preserved water rights or funding for projects designed to prevent evaporation and drain storage reservoirs. Brown says parts of Californi higher mountains still face severe and persistent drought conditions., and rains are needed soon. The governor decided that 164 of California's technical drought criteria had been met, leaving just 42 out of 415 population districts with severe problems. Those remote areas have not received recent precipitation. The weather service reported 10 inches (25 centimeters) of new fall rainfall at an area atop Buttermilk Mountain north of Sacramento this week alone. According to the Gold Country Tribune newspaper, another 50 inches (1.5 meters) may accumulate by summer's end. Lake Oroville is America's tallest man-made lake, nearly 140 feet (44",
